# Install

The installers need Node.js 26.13.0 and npm. They download the latest GitHub release tarball and install it globally, so the npm registry is not involved. No configuration is required for the first run.

## Windows

Open PowerShell and run:

<CopyCommand command={`irm ${INSTALL_PS1_URL} | iex`} label="PowerShell" />

If PowerShell blocks the script, open a new PowerShell window and run it again. The installer does not change the execution policy.

## macOS and Linux

Open a terminal and run:

<CopyCommand command={`curl -fsSL ${INSTALL_SH_URL} | sh`} label="Terminal" />

The script downloads the latest release with `npm` from your current Node.js. If `npm` is missing, install Node.js 26.13.0 first.

## Run it

```bash
last30days
```

The reader starts at `http://127.0.0.1:4477` and opens your browser. The first run uses the default password `last30`. Change it in Settings after you sign in.

If port 4477 is busy, the command tries 4477 through 4487. To pin a port:

```bash
# macOS and Linux
PORT=4480 last30days
```

```powershell
# Windows PowerShell
$env:PORT = 4480
last30days
```

`HOST` defaults to `127.0.0.1`. Keep it there unless a reverse proxy is in front of the app.

## Run without installing

```bash
npx last30days-news
```

`npx` downloads the npm package for one run and starts the same reader. It needs the package to be published on npm; the installers above use GitHub releases instead.

## Data location

The first run creates one SQLite database:

- Windows: `%APPDATA%\last30days-news`
- macOS: `~/Library/Application Support/last30days-news`
- Linux: `$XDG_DATA_HOME/last30days-news`, or `~/.local/share/last30days-news`
- Any platform: `L30_DATA_DIR` overrides the default

The database is `<data-dir>/db.sqlite`, with WAL mode and foreign keys enabled. Back up that file and you have backed up the reader.

## Command line

```text
last30days [options]

  --no-open       Do not open the browser
  --version, -v   Print the version
  --help, -h      Print help
```

Set `L30_DEBUG=1` for verbose logs. Logs are newline-delimited JSON.

